# PART 5 — SENTENCE-BY-SENTENCE READING ENGINE

## 21. DEFAULT READING BEHAVIOR

THIS IS ONE OF THE MOST IMPORTANT REQUIREMENTS.

The default reading mode must be SENTENCE BY SENTENCE.

NOT WORD BY WORD.

When the user presses Play:

1. Read the first complete German sentence.
2. Finish the complete sentence.
3. Move naturally to the next sentence.
4. Read the next complete sentence.
5. Continue automatically until the end of the text.

Do not read individual words separately during normal reading.

Do not insert artificial pauses between individual words.

The voice must maintain natural German rhythm and sentence intonation.

## 22. SENTENCE HIGHLIGHTING

During normal playback:

- Highlight the complete active sentence.
- Do not highlight individual words one by one.
- Use a subtle background highlight.
- Keep the highlighted sentence clearly visible.
- Automatically scroll to the active sentence.
- Remove the highlight when appropriate.
- Highlight the next sentence when its audio starts.

The highlighting must be synchronized with actual audio playback.

Do not use arbitrary fixed timers to simulate synchronization.

## 23. SENTENCE SEGMENTATION

Implement intelligent German sentence detection.

Recognize:
- Full stops.
- Question marks.
- Exclamation marks.
- Paragraph boundaries.
- German quotation marks.
- Abbreviations.
- Decimal numbers.
- Common German titles and abbreviations.

Avoid splitting a sentence incorrectly.

Preserve punctuation and the original text.

The application should understand German sentence structure as well as possible.

## 24. SENTENCE AUDIO GENERATION

For accurate sentence highlighting:

- Segment the text into complete sentences.
- Prepare speech audio for each sentence or use reliable sentence-level timing metadata when supported.
- Preserve the same selected German voice.
- Queue the audio in the correct order.
- Start the following sentence automatically after the previous one finishes.
- Use a small natural pause between sentences.
- Use a slightly longer pause between paragraphs.

Suggested default pauses:

- Between sentences: 250–450 milliseconds.
- Between paragraphs: 600–900 milliseconds.

Allow appropriate natural variation where useful.

Do not create awkward silence.

Do not interrupt the natural sentence flow.

## 25. LONG TEXT PLAYBACK

The application must support continuous reading of long German texts.

Target use cases:
- 10 minutes.
- 20 minutes.
- 30 minutes.
- 45 minutes.
- 60 minutes or more.

Avoid:
- Unexpected playback stops.
- Overlapping audio.
- Repeated sentences.
- Missing sentences.
- Voice changes.
- Excessive pauses.
- Unnecessary API requests.

Use audio chunking, prefetching and caching to support long sessions efficiently.

Prepare the first few sentences before playback when possible.

Prepare subsequent sentences in the background as the current ones play.

Recover gracefully from temporary network or TTS failures.

Never lose the current sentence position after a recoverable error.

